  "title": "Keep protected paths protected for patterns with a trailing slash",
  "file": "src/gates/audit.ts",
  "A": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "A repository protects its fixtures with a trailing-slash glob that previously worked. An agent edits an existing fixture, and the audit no longer blocks the protected change.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun \"${TMPDIR%/}/review-811.E2tIYC/probe.ts\"",
    "output": "F1 {\"pattern\":\"test/fixtures/**/\",\"baseMatches\":true,\"headMatches\":false,\"headProtectedFindings\":[]}\nF1 {\"pattern\":\"test/fixtures/\",\"baseMatches\":false,\"headMatches\":false,\"headProtectedFindings\":[]}\nF1 {\"pattern\":\"**/\",\"baseMatches\":true,\"headMatches\":false,\"headProtectedFindings\":[]}\nF1 {\"pattern\":\"test/fixtures/**\",\"baseMatches\":true,\"headMatches\":true,\"headProtectedFindings\":[{\"rule\":\"protected-path\",\"severity\":\"block\",\"file\":\"test/fixtures/a.json\",\"detail\":\"Edited a protected path.\"}]}"
   },
   "evidence": "The temporary probe compares the extracted base matcher with HEAD's exported matcher and calls HEAD's auditDiff for an existing-file modification. Base replaces /\\*\\*\\/?/g with a globstar placeholder, consuming the trailing slash in '**/'. HEAD instead uses 'const segments = glob.split(\"/\")'; its nonterminal '**' becomes '(?:[^/]+/)*', followed by an empty final segment. Consequently the pattern cannot match a filename. auditDiff uses this matcher for protectedPaths, so the lost match removes the blocking finding. The reviewer overstates one example: plain 'test/fixtures/' did not match descendants at base either."
  },
  "B": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "Suppose a repository declares its protected fixtures as `test/fixtures/**/` (or `**/`). An agent then edits `test/fixtures/a.json`. Before this change the audit blocked that edit; now it raises no finding at all, so the protected file can be changed without anyone being told.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun $TMPDIR/adj/f1.ts  (compares base globToRegex with head's exported globToRegex)",
    "output": "test/fixtures/**/ test/fixtures/a.json base: true head: false\ntest/fixtures/**/ test/fixtures/x/a.json base: true head: false\ntest/fixtures/ test/fixtures/a.json base: false head: false\n**/ test/fixtures/a.json base: true head: false\ntest/fixtures/** test/fixtures/a.json base: true head: true"
   },
   "evidence": "Base turned `**/` into `.*`, so `test/fixtures/**/` became `^test/fixtures/.*$`. Head splits the pattern on `/`. A `**` segment that is not last becomes `(?:[^/]+/)*`, and the empty last segment adds nothing, which gives `^test/fixtures/(?:[^/]+/)*$`. That regex matches only directory-like paths that end in `/`, never a file. audit.ts:218 builds the protected-path matchers with this same globToRegex. One note on the claim: the bare `test/fixtures/` form never matched files at base either, so only the `**/` forms regressed."
  }
 },
